@subsection The Core of the Evaluator

The evaluation process can be described as the interplay between two
procedures: @code{eval} and @code{applyProcedure}.

@subsubheading Eval

@code{Eval} takes as arguments an expression and an environment.  It classifies
the expression and directs its evaluation.  @code{Eval} is structured as a case
analysis of the syntactic type of the expression to be evaluated.  In this
edition the syntactic type is literal: the parser has already handed @code{eval}
one @code{Expr} value whose type @emph{is} the expression type, so the case
analysis is a @code{when} with one arm per node, and the compiler counts the
arms.  This @newterm{abstract syntax} is what makes it easy to see how we can
change the syntax of the language by using the same evaluator with a different
parser: @code{eval} never sees surface lists at all.

@noindent
@b{Primitive expressions}

@itemize @bullet

@item
For self-evaluating expressions, such as numbers, @code{eval} returns the
expression itself.

@item
@code{Eval} must look up variables in the environment to find their values.

@end itemize

@noindent
@b{Special forms}

@itemize @bullet

@item
For quoted expressions, @code{eval} returns the expression that was quoted.

@item
An assignment to (or a definition of) a variable must recursively call
@code{eval} to compute the new value to be associated with the variable.  The
environment must be modified to change (or create) the binding of the variable.

@item
An @code{if} expression requires special processing of its parts, so as to
evaluate the consequent if the predicate is true, and otherwise to evaluate the
alternative.

@item
A @code{lambda} expression must be transformed into an applicable procedure by
packaging together the parameters and body specified by the @code{lambda}
expression with the environment of the evaluation.

@item
A @code{begin} expression requires evaluating its sequence of expressions in
the order in which they appear.

@item
A case analysis (@code{cond}) is transformed into a nest of @code{if}
expressions and then evaluated.

@end itemize

@noindent
@b{Combinations}

@itemize @bullet

@item
For a procedure application, @code{eval} must recursively evaluate the operator
part and the operands of the combination.  The resulting procedure and
arguments are passed to @code{applyProcedure}, which handles the actual
procedure application.

@end itemize

@noindent
Here is the definition of @code{eval}.  Kotlin has no mutual tail-call
elimination, so @code{eval} is a @newterm{dispatch loop}: each round runs one
clause of the case analysis, and a clause that would recurse in a tail position
--- the chosen branch of an @code{if}, the last expression of a sequence, the
body of an applied compound procedure --- answers the next expression to run
instead of calling.  The book's tail positions are made explicit as the two
alternatives of an @code{EvalStep}:

@example kotlin
public sealed interface EvalStep @{
    public data class Done(
        val v: Value,
    ) : EvalStep

    public data class Continue(
        val expr: Expr,
        val env: Env,
    ) : EvalStep
@}

context(r: Raise<SchemeError>)
public fun eval(
    expr: Expr,
    env: Env,
): Value @{
    var e = expr
    var en = env
    while (true) @{
        when (val s = step(e, en)) @{
            is EvalStep.Done -> @{
                return s.v
            @}

            is EvalStep.Continue -> @{
                e = s.expr
                en = s.env
            @}
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The clause chain lives in @code{step} and its @code{baseStep} fallback:

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<SchemeError>)
protected fun baseStep(
    expr: Expr,
    env: Env,
): EvalStep =
    when (expr) @{
        is LitE -> EvalStep.Done(expr.v)

        // self-evaluating?
        is VarE -> EvalStep.Done(lookupVariable(expr.name, env))

        // variable?
        is QuoteE -> EvalStep.Done(expr.datum)

        // quoted?
        is SetE -> evalAssignment(expr, env)

        // assignment?
        is DefineE -> evalDefinition(expr, env)

        // definition?
        is IfE -> evalIf(expr, env)

        // if?
        is LambdaE -> EvalStep.Done(makeProcedure(expr.params, expr.rest, expr.body, env, null))

        // lambda?
        is BeginE -> evalSequence(expr.actions, env)

        // begin?
        is CondE -> EvalStep.Continue(condToIf(expr), env)

        // cond? -> derived
        is LetE -> EvalStep.Continue(letToCombination(expr), env)

        // derived
        is AppE -> evalApplication(expr, env) // application?
    @}
@end example

@noindent
For clarity, @code{eval}'s chain has been implemented as a case analysis using
a @code{when}.  The disadvantage of this is that our evaluator handles only a
few distinguishable types of expressions, and no new ones can be defined
without editing @code{baseStep}.  In most Lisp implementations, dispatching on
the type of an expression is done in a data-directed style.  This edition keeps
one deliberate seam in its place: @code{step} is an overridable member that
@code{eval} consults on every round, so an exercise can check for the forms it
adds and fall back to @code{baseStep} for everything else, and the check fires
at every nesting depth because every clause recurses through @code{eval}.  (See
@ref{Exercise 4.3} for the data-directed alternative.)  Note also that the
book's ``unknown expression type'' fault cannot fire here: the parser maps
every well-formed datum onto a typed node, so a malformed form fails at parse
time with the typed parse error of the edition's @newterm{typed errors}.

@subsubheading Apply

@code{ApplyProcedure} takes two arguments, a procedure and a list of arguments
to which the procedure should be applied.  It classifies procedures into two
kinds: a primitive applies through the runtime (we show that arm in
@ref{4.1.4}, where the book's @code{apply-primitive-procedure} lives); it
applies compound procedures by sequentially evaluating the expressions that
make up the body of the procedure.  The environment for the evaluation of the
body of a compound procedure is constructed by extending the base environment
carried by the procedure to include a frame that binds the parameters of the
procedure to the arguments to which the procedure is to be applied.  The
application clause of @code{eval} feeds it:

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<SchemeError>)
protected open fun evalApplication(
    expr: AppE,
    env: Env,
): EvalStep @{
    val procedure = eval(expr.operator, env)
    val arguments = listOfValues(expr.operands, env)
    return applyProcedure(procedure, arguments)
@}
@end example

@noindent
and the compound arm of @code{applyProcedure} evaluates all but the last body
expression and answers the last one as the next expression for the dispatch
loop:

@example kotlin
when (procedure) @{
    is VProc -> @{
        val frame = extendEnvironment(procedure.params.toList(), arguments, procedure.env, procedure.rest)
        for (i in 0 until procedure.body.size - 1) eval(procedure.body[i], frame)
        EvalStep.Continue(procedure.body.last(), frame)
    @}

    else -> @{
        r.raise(SchemeError.NotApplicable(procedure))
    @}
@}
@end example

@subsubheading Procedure arguments

When @code{eval} processes a procedure application, it uses
@code{listOfValues} to produce the list of arguments to which the procedure
is to be applied. @code{listOfValues} takes as an argument the operands of
the combination.  It evaluates each operand and returns a list of the
corresponding values:@footnote{The book's original @code{list-of-values} was
an explicit recursion, written (rather than using @code{map}) to emphasize the
fact that the evaluator can be implemented without any use of higher-order
procedures (and thus could be written in a language that doesn't have
higher-order procedures), even though the language that it supports will
include higher-order procedures.  In this edition @code{listOfValues} is the
@code{map} version the book contemplated: the host's @code{map} visits the
operands in order, which is exactly where the fixed operand order that
exercise 4.1 discusses comes from.}

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<SchemeError>)
protected open fun listOfValues(
    operands: PersistentList<Expr>,
    env: Env,
): List<Value> = operands.map @{ eval(it, env) @}
@end example

@subsubheading Conditionals

@code{evalIf} evaluates the predicate part of an @code{if} expression in the
given environment.  If the result is true, @code{evalIf} continues with the
consequent, otherwise it continues with the alternative:

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<SchemeError>)
private fun evalIf(
    expr: IfE,
    env: Env,
): EvalStep @{
    val predicate = eval(expr.predicate, env)
    return EvalStep.Continue(if (isTrue(predicate)) expr.consequent else expr.alternative, env)
@}
@end example

@noindent
The use of @code{isTrue} in @code{evalIf} highlights the issue of the
connection between an implemented language and an implementation language.  The
predicate is evaluated in the language being implemented and thus yields a
value in that language.  The interpreter predicate @code{isTrue} translates
that value into a value that can be tested by the @code{if} in the
implementation language: The metacircular representation of truth might not be
the same as that of the host, Kotlin.@footnote{Here the language being
implemented is the Scheme subset and the implementation language is Kotlin.
Contemplation of the meaning of @code{isTrue} here yields expansion of
consciousness without the abuse of substance.}

@subsubheading Sequences

@code{evalSequence} is used by @code{applyProcedure} to evaluate the sequence of
expressions in a procedure body and by @code{eval} to evaluate the sequence of
expressions in a @code{begin} expression.  It takes as arguments a sequence of
expressions and an environment, and evaluates the expressions in the order in
which they occur.  The value returned is the value of the final expression ---
in the dispatch loop, the final expression is the next tail expression, not a
recursive call:

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<SchemeError>)
private fun evalSequence(
    actions: PersistentList<Expr>,
    env: Env,
): EvalStep @{
    if (actions.isEmpty()) r.raise(SchemeError.Parse("empty sequence"))
    for (i in 0 until actions.size - 1) eval(actions[i], env)
    return EvalStep.Continue(actions.last(), env)
@}
@end example

@subsubheading Assignments and definitions

The following procedure handles assignments to variables.  It calls @code{eval}
to find the value to be assigned and transmits the variable and the resulting
value to @code{setVariable} to be installed in the designated
environment.

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<SchemeError>)
private fun evalAssignment(
    expr: SetE,
    env: Env,
): EvalStep @{
    setVariable(expr.name, eval(expr.value, env), env)
    return EvalStep.Done(VSym("ok"))
@}
@end example

@noindent
Definitions of variables are handled in a similar manner.@footnote{This
implementation of @code{define} ignores a subtle issue in the handling of
internal definitions, although it works correctly in most cases.  We will see
what the problem is and how to solve it in @ref{4.1.6}.}

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<SchemeError>)
private fun evalDefinition(
    expr: DefineE,
    env: Env,
): EvalStep @{
    val value = expr.value
    val v =
        if (value is LambdaE) @{
            makeProcedure(value.params, value.rest, value.body, env, expr.name)
        @} else @{
            eval(value, env)
        @}
    defineVariable(expr.name, v, env)
    return EvalStep.Done(VSym("ok"))
@}
@end example

@noindent
We have chosen here to return the symbol @code{ok} as the value of an
assignment or a definition.@footnote{As we said when we introduced
@code{define} and @code{set!}, these values are implementation-dependent in
Scheme---that is, the implementor can choose what value to return.}

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.1}Exercise 4.1:} This host settles the question the
original leaves open: Kotlin always evaluates a procedure's operands from left
to right, so the metacircular evaluator inherits a fixed order, and we can
already tell which one --- there is nothing to observe.  The order becomes a
property of the evaluator itself only when the evaluator's own code decides
it: write @code{listOfValues} out by hand, as an explicit recursion, and the
host stops deciding for us.  Write a version of @code{listOfValues} that
evaluates operands from left to right by construction, and a version that
evaluates them from right to left, and run the same operand-recording program
under both to see exactly what the host order had been deciding for us.
@end quotation

