@subsection Evaluator Data Structures

In addition to defining the external syntax of expressions, the evaluator
implementation must also define the data structures that the evaluator
manipulates internally, as part of the execution of a program, such as the
representation of procedures and environments and the representation of true
and false.

@subsubheading Testing of predicates

For conditionals, we accept anything to be true that is not the explicit
@code{false} object --- in this edition, the boolean value @code{VBool(false)}:

@example kotlin
public fun isTrue(v: Value): Boolean = v != VBool(false)
@end example

@subsubheading Representing procedures

To handle primitives, we assume that we have available the following
procedures:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{callPrimitive ⟨@var{proc}⟩ ⟨@var{args}⟩}

@noindent
applies the given primitive procedure to the argument values in the list
@code{⟨}@var{args}@code{⟩} and returns the result of the application.

@item
the test @code{v is VPrimitive}

@noindent
tests whether @code{⟨}@var{v}@code{⟩} is a primitive procedure.

@end itemize

@noindent
These mechanisms for handling primitives are further described in 
@ref{4.1.4}.

Compound procedures are constructed from parameters, procedure bodies, and
environments using the constructor @code{makeProcedure}:

@example kotlin
protected open fun makeProcedure(
    params: PersistentList<String>,
    rest: String?,
    body: PersistentList<Expr>,
    env: Env,
    name: String?,
): Value = VProc(params, rest, body, env, name)
@end example

@noindent
The value is a @code{VProc}: the parameter names, the body expressions, the
environment the @code{lambda} captured, and the definition name, if any.  The
printer keeps the environment part out of sight, the way the book's
@code{user-print} does (see @ref{4.1.4}): a named procedure prints as
@code{#[compound-procedure name]}, an anonymous one as
@code{#[compound-procedure]}.

@subsubheading Operations on Environments

The evaluator needs operations for manipulating environments.  As explained in
@ref{3.2}, an environment is a sequence of frames, where each frame is
a table of bindings that associate variables with their corresponding values.
We use the following operations for manipulating environments:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{lookup(⟨@var{env}⟩, ⟨@var{name}⟩)}

returns the value that is bound to the name @code{⟨}@var{name}@code{⟩} in the environment
@code{⟨}@var{env}@code{⟩}, or signals an error if the variable is unbound.

@item
@code{extend(⟨@var{names}⟩, ⟨@var{values}⟩, ⟨@var{base-env}⟩)}

returns a new environment, consisting of a new frame in which the symbols in
the list @code{⟨}@var{names}@code{⟩} are bound to the corresponding elements in the list
@code{⟨}@var{values}@code{⟩}, where the enclosing environment is the environment
@code{⟨}@var{base-env}@code{⟩}.

@item
@code{define(⟨@var{env}⟩, ⟨@var{name}⟩, ⟨@var{value}⟩)}

adds to the first frame in the environment @code{⟨}@var{env}@code{⟩} a new binding that
associates the variable @code{⟨}@var{name}@code{⟩} with the value @code{⟨}@var{value}@code{⟩}.

@item
@code{set(⟨@var{env}⟩, ⟨@var{name}⟩, ⟨@var{value}⟩)}

changes the binding of the variable @code{⟨}@var{name}@code{⟩} in the environment @code{⟨}@var{env}@code{⟩}
so that the variable is now bound to the value @code{⟨}@var{value}@code{⟩}, or signals an
error if the variable is unbound.

@end itemize

@noindent
To implement these operations we use the environments of @ref{3.2}: an
environment is a chain of frames, each frame a name-to-value table, and the
enclosing environment of an environment is the frame's parent.  There is no
empty-environment sentinel: the chain simply ends at the global frame, where
the parent is absent.  The four operations are thin members of @code{Env},
each a single step over that representation.

To extend an environment by a new frame that associates variables with values,
we make a frame binding each name to the value at the same position, and we
adjoin this to the environment.  We signal a typed error if the number of
variables does not match the number of values.

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<SchemeError>)
public fun extend(
    names: List<String>,
    values: List<Value>,
    parent: Env,
    rest: String? = null,
): Env @{
    if (rest == null && names.size != values.size) @{
        r.raise(SchemeError.WrongArity("extend", names.size.toString(), values.size))
    @}
    if (rest != null && values.size < names.size) @{
        r.raise(SchemeError.WrongArity("extend", "at least $@{names.size@}", values.size))
    @}
    val bound = names.zip(values).toMap(persistentMapOf<String, Value>().builder())
    val env = Env(bound.build(), parent)
    if (rest != null) @{
        env.frame = env.frame.putting(rest, vlist(values.drop(names.size)))
    @}
    return env
@}
@end example

@noindent
To look up a variable in an environment, we search the table of the first
frame.  If we find the desired variable, we return its value.  If we do not
find the variable in the current frame, we search the enclosing environment,
and so on.  If the chain runs out, we raise the edition's ``unbound variable''
error.

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<SchemeError>)
public fun lookup(name: String): Value @{
    var cursor: Env? = this
    while (cursor != null) @{
        val hit = cursor.frame[name]
        if (hit != null) return hit
        cursor = cursor.parent
    @}
    r.raise(SchemeError.Unbound(name))
@}
@end example

@noindent
To set a variable to a new value in a specified environment, we search for the
variable, just as in @code{lookup}, and change the corresponding value when we
find it.

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<SchemeError>)
public fun set(
    name: String,
    value: Value,
) @{
    var cursor: Env? = this
    while (cursor != null) @{
        if (cursor.frame.containsKey(name)) @{
            cursor.frame = cursor.frame.putting(name, value)
            return
        @}
        cursor = cursor.parent
    @}
    r.raise(SchemeError.Unbound(name))
@}
@end example

@noindent
To define a variable, we add the binding to the first frame's table, shadowing
any outer binding of the same name without touching it.

@example kotlin
public fun define(
    name: String,
    value: Value,
) @{
    frame = frame.putting(name, value)
@}
@end example

@noindent
Each frame is a persistent map behind a @code{var}, the frame model of the
re-cut @ref{3.2}: @code{define} rebinds the frame's map on the same node, so
every holder of the node sees the new binding, while @code{set} rebinds the map
of the frame that owns the name.@footnote{Frames are not really a data
abstraction in the following code: @code{set} and @code{define} rewrite the
frame's map directly.  The purpose of the @code{Env} operations is to make the
environment-manipulation procedures easy to read.}

The method described here is only one of many plausible ways to represent
environments.  Since we used data abstraction to isolate the rest of the
evaluator from the detailed choice of representation, we could change the
environment representation if we wanted to.  (See @ref{Exercise 4.11}.)  In a
production-quality Lisp system, the speed of the evaluator's environment
operations---especially that of variable lookup---has a major impact on the
performance of the system.  The representation described here, although
conceptually simple, is not efficient and would not ordinarily be used in a
production system.@footnote{The drawback of this representation (as well as the
variant in @ref{Exercise 4.11}) is that the evaluator may have to search
through many frames in order to find the binding for a given variable.  (Such
an approach is referred to as @newterm{deep binding}.)  One way to avoid this
inefficiency is to make use of a strategy called @newterm{lexical addressing},
which will be discussed in @ref{5.5.6}.}

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.11}Exercise 4.11:} Instead of the runtime's frame
(a name-to-value map), represent a frame as a list of bindings, where each
binding is a name-value pair.  Rewrite the environment operations to use this
alternative representation.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.12}Exercise 4.12:} The operations
@code{define}, @code{set} and
@code{lookup} can be expressed in terms of more abstract
procedures for traversing the environment structure.  Define abstractions that
capture the common patterns and redefine the three operations in terms of these
abstractions.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.13}Exercise 4.13:} Scheme allows us to create new
bindings for variables by means of @code{define}, but provides no way to get
rid of bindings.  Implement for the evaluator a special form
@code{make-unbound!} that removes the binding of a given symbol from the
environment in which the @code{make-unbound!} expression is evaluated.  This
problem is not completely specified.  For example, should we remove only the
binding in the first frame of the environment?  Complete the specification and
justify any choices you make.
@end quotation

@node	4.1.4, 4.1.5, 4.1.3, 4.1
@subsection Running the Evaluator as a Program

Given the evaluator, we have in our hands a description (expressed in Kotlin) of
the process by which the language's expressions are evaluated.  One advantage of
expressing the evaluator as a program is that we can run the program.  This
gives us, running within Kotlin, a working model of how the language itself
evaluates expressions.  This can serve as a framework for experimenting with
evaluation rules, as we shall do later in this chapter.

Our evaluator program reduces expressions ultimately to the application of
primitive procedures.  Therefore, all that we need to run the evaluator is to
create a mechanism that calls on the underlying Kotlin runtime to model the
application of primitive procedures.

There must be a binding for each primitive procedure name, so that when
@code{eval} evaluates the operator of an application of a primitive, it will
find an object to pass to @code{applyProcedure}.  We thus set up a global environment
that associates unique objects with the names of the primitive procedures that
can appear in the expressions we will be evaluating.  The global environment
also includes bindings for the symbols @code{true} and @code{false}, so that
they can be used as variables in expressions to be evaluated.

@example kotlin
public fun setupEnvironment(out: OutputSink): Env @{
    val env = Env.global()
    for (entry in defaultPrimitives(out)) @{
        env.define(entry.name, entry.toValue())
    @}
    env.define("true", VBool(true))
    env.define("false", VBool(false))
    return env
@}
@end example

@noindent
It does not matter how we represent the primitive procedure objects, so long as
@code{applyProcedure} can identify and apply them.  We have chosen to represent
a primitive procedure as a value carrying its printed name and the Kotlin
function that implements it:

@example kotlin
// a primitive procedure value: VPrimitive(name, f), where f is the
// Kotlin function applied to the argument values
@end example

@noindent
@code{setupEnvironment} will get the primitive names and implementation
functions from a table:@footnote{Any function written in Kotlin
can be used as a primitive for the metacircular evaluator.  The name of a
primitive installed in the evaluator need not be the same as the name of its
implementation in Kotlin; the names are the same here because the
metacircular evaluator implements Scheme itself.  Thus, for example, we could
put an entry named @code{first} for the function of @code{car}, or an entry
named @code{square} for a squaring function, in the table of
@code{defaultPrimitives}.}

@example kotlin
public fun defaultPrimitives(out: OutputSink): List<PrimitiveEntry> =
    listOf(
        PrimitiveEntry("car") @{ args -> pairOf("car", one("car", args)).car @},
        PrimitiveEntry("cdr") @{ args -> pairOf("cdr", one("cdr", args)).cdr @},
        PrimitiveEntry("cons") @{ args ->
            val (a, b) = two("cons", args)
            cons(a, b)
        @},
        PrimitiveEntry("null?") @{ args -> VBool(one("null?", args) is VNil) @},
        // ⟨more primitives⟩
    )
@end example

@noindent
To apply a primitive procedure, we simply apply the implementation function to
the arguments --- an ordinary Kotlin call, the role the book's
@code{apply-in-underlying-scheme} played:@footnote{The book needed
@code{apply-in-underlying-scheme} because its metacircular @code{apply} and
the host's @code{apply} shared one name, and the definition of the former
masked the latter.  In Kotlin the two never share a name: the host's
application is an ordinary call, and the metacircular @code{applyProcedure}
(@ref{4.1.1}) models its working.  There is one twist: the object language's
@code{apply} and @code{map} must apply procedure values, which a plain
primitive function cannot do, so @code{applyProcedure} intercepts those two
names and re-enters itself before the plain handler call would run.}

@example kotlin
is VPrimitive -> @{
    when (procedure.name) @{
        "apply" -> @{
            if (arguments.size != 2) @{
                r.raise(SchemeError.WrongArity("apply", "2", arguments.size))
            @}
            applyProcedure(arguments[0], listItems(arguments[1]))
        @}

        "map" -> @{
            if (arguments.size != 2) @{
                r.raise(SchemeError.WrongArity("map", "2", arguments.size))
            @}
            val items = listItems(arguments[1])
            EvalStep.Done(vlist(items.map @{ applyFully(arguments[0], listOf(it)) @}))
        @}

        else -> @{
            EvalStep.Done(callPrimitive(procedure, arguments))
        @}
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
where the helper @code{applyFully} runs the dispatch loop to a value when an
intercepted application lands in a tail position.

For convenience in running the metacircular evaluator, we provide a
@newterm{driver loop} that models the read-eval-print loop of a Lisp
system: it prints a @newterm{prompt}, reads an input expression,
evaluates this expression in the global environment, and prints the result,
preceding each printed result by an @newterm{output prompt} so as to
distinguish the value of the expression from other output.  An interleaved
prompt would bury a program's own output, so this edition's driver records a
bare transcript instead, under the one-transcript contract: a definition prints
nothing, @code{display} and @code{newline} applications write their side effect
with no value line, every other expression contributes one value line, and an
@code{Error:} line stops the program.@footnote{The reader is given code, like
the book's @code{read}: @code{readProgram} takes the program text and returns
the sequence of data the forms denote.  For example, if the text is
@code{(+ 23 x)}, the reader produces an application whose operator is the
symbol @code{+} and whose operands are the number 23 and the symbol @code{x}.
If the text is @code{'x}, it produces the quotation of the symbol @code{x}.}

@example kotlin
public fun runProgram(
    text: String,
    env: Env,
    sink: OutputSink,
): String @{
    either @{
        val forms = readProgram(text)
        val exprs = parseProgram(forms)
        for (expr in exprs) @{
            runForm(expr, env, sink)
        @}
    @}.fold(
        @{ e -> sink.line("Error: $@{formatError(e)@}") @},
        @{ @},
    )
    return sink.toString()
@}
@end example

@noindent
The shared printer plays the role of the book's @code{user-print}, avoiding the
printing of the environment part of a compound procedure, which may be a very
large structure (or may even contain cycles):

@example kotlin
runProgram(
    """
    (define (square x) (* x x))
    square
    """.trimIndent(),
)
// => #[compound-procedure square]
@end example

@noindent
Now all we need to do to run the evaluator is to set up the global environment
and run a program in it.  Here is a sample interaction:

@example kotlin
runProgram(
    """
    (define (append x y)
      (if (null? x)
          y
          (cons (car x) (append (cdr x) y))))
    (append '(a b c) '(d e f))
    """.trimIndent(),
)
// => (a b c d e f)
@end example

@noindent
The definition prints nothing; the application's value line is the transcript's
only line.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.14}Exercise 4.14:} Eva Lu Ator and Louis Reasoner
are each experimenting with the metacircular evaluator.  Eva types in the
definition of @code{map}, and runs some test programs that use it.  They work
fine.  Louis, in contrast, has installed the system version of @code{map} as a
primitive for the metacircular evaluator.  When he tries it, things go terribly
wrong.  Explain why Louis's @code{map} fails even though Eva's works.
@end quotation

@node	4.1.5, 4.1.6, 4.1.4, 4.1
@subsection Data as Programs

In thinking about a Kotlin program that evaluates Lisp expressions, an analogy
might be helpful.  One operational view of the meaning of a program is that a
program is a description of an abstract (perhaps infinitely large) machine.
For example, consider the familiar program to compute factorials:

@example kotlin
runProgram(
    """
    (define (factorial n)
      (if (= n 1)
          1
          (* (factorial (- n 1)) n)))
    (factorial 5)
    """.trimIndent(),
)
// => 120
@end example

@noindent
We may regard this program as the description of a machine containing parts
that decrement, multiply, and test for equality, together with a two-position
switch and another factorial machine. (The factorial machine is infinite
because it contains another factorial machine within it.)  @ref{Figure 4.2} is
a flow diagram for the factorial machine, showing how the parts are wired
together.

@float
@anchor{Figure 4.2}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 4.2:} The factorial program, viewed as an abstract machine.

@example
    +-----------------------------------+
    | factorial                   |1    |
    |              |1             V     |
    |              |           +-----+  |
    |              V           | #   |  |
    |           +-----+        |     |  |
6 --------*-----|  =  |------->|   #-+-----> 720
    |     |     +-----+        |  /  |  |
    |     |                    | #   |  |
    |     |                    +-----+  |
    |     |                       ^     |
    |     |                       |     |
    |     |                    +--+--+  |
    |     *------------------->|  *  |  |
    |     |                    +-----+  |
    |     V                       ^     |
    |  +-----+    +-----------+   |     |
    |  |  -  +--->| factorial +---+     |
    |  +-----+    +-----------+         |
